In article <GRABINER.92Feb20230751@zariski.harvard.edu> grabiner@math.harvard.edu (David Grabiner) writes:
>In article <1992Feb20.165000.8541@ucunix.san.uc.edu>, Matthew G. Lovell writes:
>> The coolest thing just happened. I was playing a Barbarian(and still am)
>> and am on the first level. I go into a room with an altar, so after
>> I kill a jackal and have a snack, I have in my possesion a partially-
>> eaten jackal corpse. So I decide "Let's offer it to the gods." Why not?
>> So I do and he/she/it enjoys it too and leaves an object at my feet.
>> I pick up the object to discover it is a broadsword called Stormbringer(!).
>I would consider this a bug; the gods should only accept unblemished
>sacrifices, not partly eaten ones.

Not really a bug.  The idea is that the game keeps track of how long an corpse
has been dead.  but, when you make it partially-eaten you reset that clock, so
you have another chance.  Heck, I have sacrificed zombies and mummies using
this method.

Happy Hacking!

